<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="title">
 <div><p class="eyebrow">Systems / field notes 01</p><h1 id="title">Keeping the<br />desktop<br /><em>within reach.</em></h1><p class="lede">A machine can finish the work and still be difficult to use. Here’s how I control memory on BOX—and what the evidence actually supports.</p><a class="read-link" href="#chapters">Read the complete guide <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a></div>
 <aside class="hero-record"><p class="eyebrow">The observation / 10 Nov 2026</p><p class="hero-value">602<span>requests. No timeouts.</span></p><p>Two one-minute windows. The slowest compositor reply was <b>4.063 ms</b>.</p><p class="limit"><b>Evidence boundary</b><br />Display off. Existing CPU job and browser batch. No completed-build comparison or visible-frame measurement.</p></aside>
</section>
<section class="position"><p class="eyebrow">The decision</p><h2>Keep the layers.<br />Test the numbers.</h2><p>Coordinate heavy starts. Attribute memory to jobs. Keep kernel reclaim and compressed swap. Preserve the control surface. Then tune from representative observations—not from a free-RAM meter.</p></section>
<section class="exhibit" id="observations"><div class="exhibit-head"><p class="eyebrow">01 / Measured latency</p><h2>Fast typical replies.<br />A variable tail.</h2><p>Same 0–5 millisecond scale for both windows. Nothing was changed between them.</p></div><div class="chart-content">
 <div class="chart-label">95th percentile</div><div class="barrow"><span>First minute</span><div class="track"><i style="width:3.4093%"></i></div><b>0.170 ms</b></div><div class="barrow"><span>Repeat</span><div class="track"><i class="accent" style="width:3.9850%"></i></div><b>0.199 ms</b></div>
 <div class="chart-label">Slowest sampled reply</div><div class="barrow"><span>First minute</span><div class="track"><i style="width:40.7483%"></i></div><b>2.037 ms</b></div><div class="barrow"><span>Repeat</span><div class="track"><i class="accent" style="width:81.2562%"></i></div><b>4.063 ms</b></div><div class="axis"><span>0</span><span>5 milliseconds</span></div>
 <details><summary>How to read this graph</summary><p>301 actual requests per window, nominally 5 Hz, with a 500 ms socket timeout. Bars start at zero. p95 uses sorted index floor((n−1) × 0.95). These are consecutive uncontrolled windows, not baseline/treatment trials.</p><p>First median/p95/p99/max: 0.137/0.170/0.196/2.037 ms. Repeat: 0.145/0.199/1.744/4.063 ms. No errors. All display-on flags were false.</p></details>
 </div></section>
<section class="trace-section"><div class="exhibit-head"><p class="eyebrow">02 / All 602 observations</p><h2>Keep the outliers<br />in the picture.</h2><p>Each dot is a recorded reply. Both panels share 0–60 seconds horizontally and 0–5 ms vertically.</p></div><div id="traces"><p class="fine">The complete numeric samples are available in the download below. JavaScript adds the two plotted traces.</p></div><a href="/guides/box-ram/measurements.json" download>Download the numeric measurements (JSON) ↗</a></section>
<section class="exhibit"><div class="exhibit-head"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Compressed storage</p><h2>Less space.<br />Not a speed-up claim.</h2><p>A snapshot of the same page contents before compression and their physical allocation in zram.</p></div><div class="chart-content"><div class="barrow"><span>Original pages</span><div class="track"><i style="width:98.05%"></i></div><b>4.90 GiB</b></div><div class="barrow"><span>RAM occupied</span><div class="track"><i class="accent" style="width:27.44%"></i></div><b>1.37 GiB</b></div><div class="axis"><span>0</span><span>5 GiB</span></div><p><b>3.53 GiB less storage</b> for these contents, including zram allocation overhead.</p><details><summary>Calculation and limits</summary><p>Source: an earlier November 10 zram mm_stat snapshot. Original data: 5,264,244,736 bytes. Total physical allocation: 1,473,019,904 bytes. GiB = bytes ÷ 2³⁰. Both bars start at zero and share a 0–5 GiB scale.</p><p>This is not a measured whole-host no-swap counterfactual, nor a measurement of compression CPU cost, application resume latency or sustainable capacity. Later snapshots differ.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="mechanism"><p class="eyebrow">04 / The mechanism</p><h2>Control the start.<br />Observe the consequence.</h2><div class="steps"><div><span>01</span><h3>Admit</h3><p>Two participating job slots, project turns, headroom and pressure checks.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Attribute</h3><p>A separate scope for supported new host jobs. Actual ancestry determines inherited limits.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Record</h3><p>A completion receipt captures peaks, pressure and exit status.</p></div><div><span>04</span><h3>Decide</h3><p>Use the evidence to test future policy. No continuous resizing controller is installed.</p></div></div><p class="fine">Logical control sequence, not a capacity chart. MGLRU, zram, disk fallback and recovery preferences operate beneath this workflow. Unwrapped jobs remain outside admission; containers may use inherited placement.</p></section>
<section class="change-log"><p class="eyebrow">05 / What changed</p><h2>Better structure.<br />Honest claims.</h2><dl><div><dt>Implemented · Nov 9</dt><dd>Job scopes, before/after telemetry and receipts; T3’s oomd preference set to avoid. Placement and lifecycle behavior were exercised. No desktop speed-up was established.</dd></div><div><dt>Measured · Nov 10</dt><dd>602 compositor replies under existing load. The controlled build timed out in admission. No RAM, CPU, concurrency or reclaim setting was changed by that measurement.</dd></div><div><dt>This guide</dt><dd>A complete reading edition, three numeric exhibits, a mechanism explanation, source notes and a public numeric dataset. Local host paths and private queue details are omitted from downloads.</dd></div><div><dt>Still proposed</dt><dd>Matched trials around an admitted build, entry-point coverage, consistent project identities and measured soft thresholds. These are future experiments, not installed improvements.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="chapters" id="chapters"><div class="chapter-intro"><p class="eyebrow">The complete guide / six chapters</p><h2>From research<br />to operating practice.</h2><p>Read one chapter at a time, or expand the full guide to search and print it.</p><div class="tools"><button id="expand" type="button">Expand chapters</button><button id="collapse" type="button">Collapse chapters</button><button id="print" type="button">Print guide</button></div></div><div class="chapter-list">{content.map(([, body], i) => <details class="chapter" id={`chapter-${i}`}><summary><span>{String(i).padStart(2,'0')}</span>{titles[i]}</summary><article set:html={body} /></details>)}</div></section>
<section class="sources" id="sources"><p class="eyebrow">Sources / methods / provenance</p><h2>A local investigation,<br />with inspectable limits.</h2><p>The chapters retain their original research links. Links to private local implementation and rollout records resolve to this source note in the public edition. Those records are summarized here; local filesystem paths and private runtime snapshots are not published.</p><p>Local records dated November 9–10 document scope placement, command exit/signal propagation, wrapper-death and descendant-lease behavior, contained reclaim checks and activation. They establish implementation behavior in those scenarios, not desktop improvement or future OOM victim choice.</p><p>The numeric dataset contains only relative sample time, reply time, wake-up lateness and summary statistics; sample values are rounded to six decimals for publication. No job identifiers, process arguments, account data or host environment snapshot is included.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~dskarlat/publications/tmo_asplos22.pdf">TMO / Senpai · ASPLOS 2022</a></li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.02922">Memory at Your Service / Hermes · author manuscript</a></li><li><a href="https://docs.kernel.org/accounting/psi.html">Linux pressure stall information</a></li><li><a href="https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html">Linux cgroup v2</a></li><li><a href="https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/blockdev/zram.html">Linux zram</a></li></ul><p class="fine">Design owner: Rahil Design QSL 5.0.0 Correspondence. Archivo and Gloock are self-hosted under SIL OFL. This is an independent personal investigation, not a universal tuning recipe or a claim of authoring the cited research.</p></section>
